      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to find the area of the two sectors and then subtract the area of the overlapping quadrilateral. The central angles of both sectors are \\(60^\\circ\\), and the radius of the circle is \\(12\\).\n\nThe formula for the area of a sector of a circle with radius \\(r\\) and central angle \\(\\theta\\) (in degrees) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360} \\times \\pi r^2\n\\]\n\nFor the sectors:\n\n1. The area of one sector:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60}{360} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n\\]\n\nSince there are two such sectors, the combined area of both sectors is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of both sectors} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the area of the overlapping quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\). To do this, we will use geometric properties and symmetry.\n\nConsider the quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\). It is composed of two overlapping 60-degree sectors with a common 60-degree angle between them. The overlapping part can be seen as two identical isosceles triangles with a vertex angle of \\(60^\\circ\\).\n\nWe can split the problem by considering the geometry of the figure and the fact that it is composed of two identical isosceles triangles sharing a common vertex (which is angle \\(BAC = 60^\\circ\\)) and an angle of \\(120^\\circ\\) at the center of the circle (which forms the other two angles when considering the total \\(60^\\circ\\) of each sector).\n\nThe overlapping area of the two triangles (which is a common part of the figure) can be calculated as follows:\n\nFor an isosceles triangle with a vertex angle of \\(60^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area of an isosceles triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times r^2 \\times \\sin(\\text{vertex angle})\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sin(60^\\circ) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the isosceles triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12^2 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 144 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{144\\sqrt{3}}{4} = 36\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nAs the overlapping quadrilateral consists of two such triangles, the total overlapping area (considering only half due to symmetry and each is split into two triangles for the given sector area calculation):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total overlapping area} = 2 \\times 36\\sqrt{3} = 72\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is the combined area minus the overlapping region:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of figure } ABCD = \\text{Total area of both sectors} - \\text{Total overlapping area}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Area of figure } ABCD = 48\\pi - 72\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi - 72\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
